6.PROOF OF NEGLIGENCE; THE USE OF CUSTOM AND EXPERT TESTIMONY

a.General

Determining what knowledge a reasonable person would have and evaluating any particular risk as one that a reasonable person would or would not undertake are normally questions for the trier of fact ultimately to determine. In helping the trier of fact make these determinations, certain kinds of evidence may be both relevant and useful. One such kind of evidence is that of custom. “Custom” is a broad term that encompasses not only the unwritten but generally prevailing practices in a community or industry, but also trade rules or standards that have been explicitly adopted by a particular profession or industry. Unless a custom is of such prevalence that the jury may be entitled to take note of it of their own accord, it is normally necessary to prove the existence of a custom through persons so familiar with the custom and its scope of application that they can be considered experts with regard to it.

One way of defining an expert, in the context of an action for personal injuries, is to say that the expert is a person who is able to testify before the jury on matters of fact within a special field of knowledge and to express an opinion on a variety of matters. Normally, witnesses are only permitted to testify regarding what they have seen or heard or otherwise directly experienced themselves. There are inevitably, however, some exceptions. For example, the ordinary person, when called as a witness, is usually permitted to testify that someone was “drunk.” Likewise a witness is generally permitted to estimate that the defendant’s car, immediately before the accident, was travelling at, say, 30 miles an hour. The questions of whether the defendant was intoxicated or of how fast he was driving his car are, in a sense, questions of fact; but they are not normally directly observable phenomena. In permitting the ordinary person to testify as to these matters, the witness is in reality being permitted to express an opinion based upon certain observed facts. The same considerations apply
256
when a witness is permitted to testify as to the reputation of one of the litigants in his community. But, with the exception of these and other similar instances, the ordinary person is not permitted to offer an opinion on the matters in issue. The rationale is that the jury is as capable as the witness of drawing the proper conclusion and that to permit the witness to evaluate the evidence is to invade the province of the jury. Nevertheless, the expert is permitted to present his opinions on a variety of matters to the jury because of the expert’s special knowledge and experience which is normally not available to the jury.

In the use of expert testimony the first requirement is the qualification of the witness as an expert. This preliminary qualification procedure is carried on before the judge, and is sometimes called the voir dire. The witness testifies as to her qualifications and the judge ultimately decides whether the witness is in fact an expert; and, if so, what she is an expert on. Following a trilogy of United States Supreme Court cases in the 1990s,8 trial judges in both federal and state courts also assess whether the expert’s opinions are reliable. If the judge concludes some opinions are not reliable the expert will not be permitted to offer those opinions. In Chapter 5 and Chapter 9 we explore the issue of reliability at greater depth.

In the current context, a typical use of an expert is to establish the custom of an industry. This sort of testimony is in a sense testimony about matters of “fact” but, as in the case of testimony about whether a person was drunk in the absence of blood tests or breath analysis, the presenting of such testimony actually requires the witness to make a number of evaluations. Although a jury is free to ignore or disagree with an expert’s opinion, it is the case that in many areas including the area of medical malpractice, that in the absence of some expert testimony supporting the plaintiff’s position in a medical malpractice case, the plaintiff normally will not be able to survive a motion for a directed verdict.

